Geographic Opportunity Analysis

The current major development step is identifying meters near each historical meter-change event.

A Haversine distance calculation is being used to calculate geographic distance between meters.

The target radius discussed is approximately:

0.5 mile

The analysis initially attempted to compare historical meters against the meter population using latitude/longitude bounds followed by the Haversine calculation.

Example concept:

Changed Meter A

→ identify meters geographically near A
→ determine whether those meters existed/current on A’s removal date
→ calculate exact distance
→ retain meters within the desired radius.

⸻

Performance Problem Encountered

The geographic join became very slow when comparing the historical meter-change population against the full meter population.

This occurs because comparing every meter against every other meter creates an extremely large number of potential meter pairs.

To reduce the search space, geographic grid fields were introduced:

* lat_grid
* lon_grid

using calculations similar to:

FLOOR(latitude / 0.02)

and

FLOOR(longitude / 0.02)

The intention is to use geographic grid cells/bounding boxes to identify only plausible nearby candidates BEFORE performing the more expensive Haversine calculation.

Latitude/longitude bounding conditions such as approximately ±0.01 or ±0.02 degrees have also been tested.

The optimization strategy is:

Full meter population

→ geographic/grid candidate filtering

→ historical-date eligibility filtering

→ Haversine calculation

→ final 0.5-mile population.

This is where development was largely paused.

